// GitHub issue #453: the three properties this kernel's unsynchronized
// state is safe BECAUSE OF, named so that removing one is a compile error
// rather than a race.
//
// Most of `kernel/` shares mutable state with no lock. That is not an
// oversight and not a convention anyone has to remember -- it is correct,
// today, because there is exactly one kernel execution context. Three
// separate facts make that true, and two of them are scheduled to be
// removed on purpose:
//
//   1. ONE CORE runs kernel code. Core 1 reaches EL1, proves it can see
//      the shared page table, and parks (GitHub issue #447 changes this).
//
//   2. KERNEL MODE DOES NOT PREEMPT. A timer interrupt taken at EL1 --
//      inside a syscall -- only sets a flag; the switch happens on the way
//      out, in kernel_process_syscall_return_schedule. The line that does
//      it is `if (lower_el)` in each platform's irq dispatch. So a syscall
//      handler runs to completion. This is Linux's CONFIG_PREEMPT_NONE.
//
//   3. INTERRUPT HANDLERS ENTER NO LOCKED SUBSYSTEM. Device handlers
//      publish and return: virtio_net_irq_handler sets two flags and calls
//      interrupt_notify(), and the real work happens later in
//      syscall-dispatch context. None of them reaches a pool, the fd
//      table, or the network stack.
//
//      The SCHEDULER is the exception, and it is not a small one:
//      kernel_process_timer_schedule is `!{interrupt}` and walks the
//      process chain and writes saved_sp, and kernel_process_uart_wake
//      writes into a blocked process's saved frame. So this leg is "no
//      handler enters a subsystem that will have a lock", not "no handler
//      touches shared state" -- the scheduler's state is shared, and is
//      unsynchronized for the same reason as everything else here rather
//      than for a different one.
//
//      NOTHING CHECKS THIS. It is tempting to say the effect system does,
//      since `locks` is rejected on an `!{interrupt}` function -- but that
//      rejection fires only on the ANNOTATION, and no function in this
//      kernel declares `!{locks}`. An interrupt handler calling
//      pool_lock_acquire today compiles. GitHub issue #449 owns both the
//      rule and the annotations that would give it teeth.
//
// Legs 1 and 2 are numbers, declared here, asserted at every site whose
// correctness argument names them. Raising either one fails the build at
// each such site -- which turns issue #447 into a worklist instead of a
// silent change of meaning. Leg 3 has no number and no check; see below.
//
// Current limitations (2026-08-27). Keep this list current as call sites
// change; a limitation that goes stale here is worse than none, since it
// invites relying on a guarantee that quietly stopped holding.
// - THE ASSERTION SET IS A CLAIM, NOT A PROOF. Nothing checks that every
//   site depending on these numbers actually asserts them. The audit
//   behind the current set is recorded in issue #453; a new piece of
//   unsynchronized shared state added without an assertion is exactly as
//   invisible as it was before this file existed. What this buys is that
//   the sites already known are known to the COMPILER instead of to a
//   comment.
// - THESE ARE NOT A DESIGN. They describe what is true, so that changing
//   it is loud. Deciding what each subsystem should do once a number
//   moves is issues #451/#452/#449, not this file.
// - LEG 3 HAS NO NUMBER AND, TODAY, NO CHECK. The `locks` effect exists
//   and its interrupt exclusion works when a function declares it; zero
//   functions do, so the rule currently forbids an annotation nobody
//   writes. Until #449 annotates the lock primitives, leg 3 is a property
//   of what the handlers happen to call, which is exactly the kind of
//   accident this file exists to stop relying on silently.

// How many cores execute kernel code. Not how many cores exist, and not
// how many the platform brings up -- core 1 boots today and this is still
// 1, because it parks without touching kernel state.
const KERNEL_ACTIVE_CORES: usize = 1;

// Whether a syscall handler can lose the CPU before it returns. 0 today:
// see leg 2 above. A separate number from the core count because the two
// are independently removable and each breaks a different set of sites --
// preemption alone would make two PROCESSES interleave inside the kernel
// on one core, which most of the state below is equally unready for.
const KERNEL_PREEMPTIBLE: usize = 0;
